+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ the fused write at two ranks
+ *
+ * ar_hc_write AND ar_gather_hc_write ON REAL PEERS. Both are the two-rank exact wire with the
+ * write as the epilogue, so what a single rank cannot check is the whole of them: the handshake,
+ * the message the reduction lands on, and whether the write sits on the message that was actually
+ * reduced. A pair is the smallest width at which any of that exists.
+ *
+ * EACH LAUNCH HAS ITS OWN BUFFERS AND NOTHING SYNCHRONISES BETWEEN THEM. The instance is shared
+ * and its slots are double-buffered by the launch parity (r4d_ar_seq.h), so a sweep that waited on
+ * the host between launches would never have two handshakes in flight and would not exercise the
+ * thing that breaks: a slot taken per block instead of per launch. Every launch is followed only
+ * by the next one; the stream is synchronised once, after the last, and every output is read back
+ * then.
+ *
+ * THE ORACLE IS THE UNFUSED CHAIN, WRITTEN FROM THE ARITHMETIC AND NOT FROM THE KERNELS: the
+ * gather's slots are summed in ascending order and narrowed with round-to-nearest-even, the two
+ * ranks' messages are added in fp32 and narrowed the same way, and the write then accumulates
+ * h + inj*y and narrows with the half-away rounding hc_write itself uses. The operands are sized
+ * so that everything up to and including the reduced message is exactly representable in bf16, so
+ * the oracle does not depend on an unstated rounding mode: the WRITE's narrowing is the one a
+ * rounding mode decides, and it is checked. The operands themselves are generated by one function
+ * that BOTH the upload and the oracle call, so the check never compares the output against a
+ * second guess at the input.
+ *
+ * NO SHARED ARM: `shared`/`shared_gate` stay absent, which keeps the sigmoid out of the oracle.
+ * The gather's routing and its weights are nonuniform, which is the half that has an arithmetic.
+ */
